<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matilda of Tuscany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1046">1046</a>–<a href="/years/1115">1115</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Matilda of Canossa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matilda of Canossa was margravine of Tuscany and ruler of wide lands in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, Reggio, Mantua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. A firm supporter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a>, she hosted the meeting at Canossa in 1077 where Emperor Henry IV did penance before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>. After her death in 1115 many of her cities organised themselves as communes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany.jpg" alt="Matilda of Tuscany" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Donizo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mathilde_und_Hugo_von_Cluny_als_F%C3%BCrsprecher_Heinrichs_IV._(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its rise as a self-governing city followed the death of <b>Matilda of Canossa</b>, Margravine of Tuscany, in 1115.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ferrara was held by <b>Matilda of Canossa</b>, who besieged it in 1101 to put down a revolt.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-ferrara">Commune of Ferrara</a></li><li><blockquote>Mantua had been a centre of <b>Matilda of Canossa's</b> lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-mantua">Commune of Mantua</a></li><li><blockquote>Modena had belonged to the lands of <b>Matilda of Canossa</b>, and its cathedral was begun in 1099 by the architect Lanfranco with sculpture by Wiligelmo.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-modena">Commune of Modena</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1027 the house of Canossa held the march; <b>Countess Matilda</b> (ruled 1076–1115) hosted the meeting at Canossa in 1077 where Emperor Henry IV sought Pope Gregory VII's forgiveness, and fought for the papacy for decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-tuscany">March of Tuscany</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mantua. Died in Bondeno di Roncore, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.93,10.84&amp;year=1090" aria-label="Bondeno di Roncore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4808 577 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Matilda of Tuscany"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5258 847h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5258 847h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=44.93,10.84&amp;year=1090">What was here in 1090? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
